    {
      "id": "N6xfBMn-0VK-02KyasDtd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 399.95523829882814,
      "y": 782.25,
      "width": 644.6395874023438,
      "height": 800,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "az",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 194998091,
      "version": 1299,
      "versionNonce": 540192229,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1794492210715,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "On-chip vs off-chip: everything inside the GPU chip box is on-chip.\nGlobal memory is DRAM on the card, outside the chip. \n\nGlobal memory: big but slow. Hundreds of clock cycles per\naccess, limited bandwidth, and every SM shares the same path\nto it.\n\nWhy simple kernels are slow: thousands of threads all\nreach for global memory at once. Traffic jams. Threads wait,\ncores sit idle.\n\nRegisters: private to one thread. Fastest memory there is. \nA kernel's local variables live here.\n\nShared memory: shared by the threads in one block. Fast but\nsmall. Gone when the block finishes.\n\nCaches: L1 is per SM, L2 is one for the whole chip. Both keep\ncopies of recently used global memory data. Automatic,\nthe kernel does not manage them.\n\nWho can see what: registers = one thread. Shared\nmemory = one block. Global memory = every thread, plus the\nhost (how data gets in and out).\n\nRule of thumb: the closer the memory sits to the cores,\nthe faster and smaller it is. registers -> shared memory ->\nL1- > L2 -> global.\n\nThe chapter 5 trick: copy what a block needs from global\nmemory into shared memory once, reuse it many times, \nwrite the result back. Fewer trips to global memory.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "On-chip vs off-chip: everything inside the GPU chip box is on-chip.\nGlobal memory is DRAM on the card, outside the chip. \n\nGlobal memory: big but slow. Hundreds of clock cycles per\naccess, limited bandwidth, and every SM shares the same path\nto it.\n\nWhy simple kernels are slow: thousands of threads all\nreach for global memory at once. Traffic jams. Threads wait,\ncores sit idle.\n\nRegisters: private to one thread. Fastest memory there is. \nA kernel's local variables live here.\n\nShared memory: shared by the threads in one block. Fast but\nsmall. Gone when the block finishes.\n\nCaches: L1 is per SM, L2 is one for the whole chip. Both keep\ncopies of recently used global memory data. Automatic,\nthe kernel does not manage them.\n\nWho can see what: registers = one thread. Shared\nmemory = one block. Global memory = every thread, plus the\nhost (how data gets in and out).\n\nRule of thumb: the closer the memory sits to the cores,\nthe faster and smaller it is. registers -> shared memory ->\nL1- > L2 -> global.\n\nThe chapter 5 trick: copy what a block needs from global\nmemory into shared memory once, reuse it many times, \nwrite the result back. Fewer trips to global memory.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
